WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.458
SPEAKER 1: This
week, we're talking

00:00:01.458 --> 00:00:04.640
about computing inside a
container, around the globe,

00:00:04.640 --> 00:00:06.230
and across the stars.

00:00:06.230 --> 00:00:08.660
Welcome to "This Week in
Cloud," where we serve you

00:00:08.660 --> 00:00:10.790
the lowest-latency news.

00:00:10.790 --> 00:00:12.980
If you're already building
an app with containers,

00:00:12.980 --> 00:00:15.940
you know there's usually more
than just your code running.

00:00:15.940 --> 00:00:17.780
Google Cloud's
Kubernetes apps gives you

00:00:17.780 --> 00:00:20.810
a simple integrated way
to incorporate open source

00:00:20.810 --> 00:00:23.670
and commercial offerings
into your environment.

00:00:23.670 --> 00:00:25.840
This means that you can
deploy your app faster,

00:00:25.840 --> 00:00:28.250
thanks to pre-built
deployment templates, default

00:00:28.250 --> 00:00:30.830
configurations, and support
for popular packaging

00:00:30.830 --> 00:00:32.840
formats like Helm charts.

00:00:32.840 --> 00:00:35.180
Build your container once
and deploy your full app

00:00:35.180 --> 00:00:37.490
anywhere with Kubernetes apps.

00:00:37.490 --> 00:00:40.340
Our Google Cloud Osaka
Region is now live and ready

00:00:40.340 --> 00:00:41.580
for business.

00:00:41.580 --> 00:00:44.540
It's our second in Japan,
seventh in Asia-Pacific,

00:00:44.540 --> 00:00:46.310
and 20th region overall.

00:00:46.310 --> 00:00:49.130
And the benefits aren't
limited to just Osaka.

00:00:49.130 --> 00:00:52.010
A second region in Japan
means lower latency and higher

00:00:52.010 --> 00:00:54.920
availability for
existing users in Tokyo.

00:00:54.920 --> 00:00:57.290
Read more about how this
is helping businesses

00:00:57.290 --> 00:01:00.860
and see which regions we've
got coming up next on the blog.

00:01:00.860 --> 00:01:03.130
BigQuery is great for
analyzing big data,

00:01:03.130 --> 00:01:06.600
and it's hard to find bigger
data than astronomy data sets.

00:01:06.600 --> 00:01:09.615
BigQuery's GIS tools aren't
just limited to Earth,

00:01:09.615 --> 00:01:10.990
as you can read
about in the blog

00:01:10.990 --> 00:01:14.050
post from Dr. Ross Thomson,
Google Cloud's solution

00:01:14.050 --> 00:01:15.250
architect.

00:01:15.250 --> 00:01:17.470
Ross talks about how
these astronomy data sets

00:01:17.470 --> 00:01:20.200
can be analyzed, and how
BigQuery's built-in tools

00:01:20.200 --> 00:01:23.050
helped make it possible
to query the stars.

00:01:23.050 --> 00:01:24.250
This week is KubeCon.

00:01:24.250 --> 00:01:26.440
And if you're there, come
check out all the Googlers

00:01:26.440 --> 00:01:28.090
talking about Kubernetes.

00:01:28.090 --> 00:01:29.740
If you couldn't make
it, don't worry,

00:01:29.740 --> 00:01:32.140
because we'll have lots of
video content and write-ups

00:01:32.140 --> 00:01:33.590
after the event.

00:01:33.590 --> 00:01:35.530
You can also stay informed
with the Kubernetes

00:01:35.530 --> 00:01:39.460
podcast, a weekly chat
about all things Kubernetes.

00:01:39.460 --> 00:01:41.350
Last week they talked
about what Anthos

00:01:41.350 --> 00:01:44.727
can do for your operations
teams, so make sure to tune in.

00:01:44.727 --> 00:01:46.810
If you want to read more
about these announcements

00:01:46.810 --> 00:01:49.390
or see a full list,
check out the link.

00:01:49.390 --> 00:01:50.980
Have any thoughts or ideas?

00:01:50.980 --> 00:01:52.690
Leave us a comment or tweet us.

00:01:52.690 --> 00:01:54.910
We'll see you in the cloud.

